emacs-jieba-rs NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

Copyright (C) 2026 Bingshan Chang <chang@bingshan.org>
See the end of the file for license conditions.

These notes cover the Emacs package 'jieba-rs' and its Rust dynamic module.
Both use the same package version, independently of the upstream jieba-rs
library version.

* Changes in emacs-jieba-rs 0.2.0 (2026-09-17)

** The native module now uses upstream jieba-rs 0.11.0.
This updates the segmentation library from 0.10.3 in our previous release.  It
includes fixes for connected letter and digit runs, and optimizations to
dictionary loading, segmentation, part-of-speech tagging, and keyword
extraction.  See the upstream release notes:
https://github.com/messense/jieba-rs/releases/tag/v0.10.4
https://github.com/messense/jieba-rs/releases/tag/v0.11.0

The requirements remain GNU Emacs 30.1 with dynamic module support and, when
building from source, Rust 1.88 or later.

** New user option 'jieba-rs-max-display-line-length'.
Automatic word boundary and part-of-speech displays now skip lines longer than
10,000 characters by default, to keep editing responsive.  The limit excludes
the terminating newline and respects narrowing.  Customize this option to change
the limit, or set it to nil to display lines of any length.  Word motion and
explicit segmentation commands continue to process the full text.

** Word motion and displays reuse line segmentation results.
Repeated movement and display refreshes reuse cached results until the text,
segmentation settings, or shared dictionary changes.  Word boundaries and
part-of-speech displays share tagged results when both are enabled.  Keyword
extractors are also reused between calls.

** Displays follow all windows showing a buffer.
Visible ranges from multiple windows are combined without duplicate annotations.
Displays refresh after scrolling, resizing, narrowing, or widening, and skip
invisible text such as folded sections.

** Indirect buffers have independent display state.
Cloned indirect buffers no longer share overlays or refresh timers.  Editing
shared text through either a base buffer or an indirect buffer refreshes the
displays in the other buffers, even when the buffer used for the edit has no
display enabled.

** Enabled displays reflect configuration and dictionary changes.
Changes to HMM use, normalization rules, boundary separators, and the line
length limit refresh active displays.  Adding words or reloading a dictionary
refreshes displays in all affected visible buffers.  Normalization rules now
select the current major mode's entry, with the t entry used as a fallback.

** Display cleanup and part-of-speech annotations are more reliable.
The final word on a line receives its part-of-speech annotation.  Delayed
refreshes act on their original buffer.  Changing major modes or encountering a
segmentation error clears obsolete or partial displays, and empty displays can
still be toggled off.

** Sentence movement stops at sentence boundaries.
Forward movement reaches the accessible buffer end for an unterminated final
sentence.  Backward movement stops at the sentence start rather than its
preceding punctuation, and no longer moves forward through leading whitespace.

** New command 'jieba-rs-reload-user-dict'.
An unchanged user dictionary is loaded only once per session, even when several
buffers use it.  This command forces a reload.  A failed load leaves the
dictionary and existing segmentation results intact, and a later load can retry
it.

** Explicit part-of-speech tags replace previous tags.
Adding an existing word with a tag, or loading a dictionary entry for it, now
updates the tag shown by part-of-speech displays.

** User dictionary validation is stricter.
Dictionary words containing NUL and excessive individual or cumulative user
frequencies are rejected before changing the dictionary.  Saved words and tags
must be single fields; a saved tag must be nonempty or omitted.  Invalid
persistence settings are checked before adding the word to the session.
Appending to a file without a final newline now keeps the new entry on a
separate line.

** Native string conversion preserves character positions.
Unicode text, including embedded NUL, is preserved during segmentation and
tagging.  Non-ASCII unibyte strings and non-Unicode Emacs characters are
rejected.  Decode raw non-ASCII bytes before passing them to the native module.
Pure ASCII unibyte strings remain supported.

** Keyword extraction handles empty results and large counts safely.
Empty input and a zero keyword count return an empty vector after argument
validation.  Result allocation is bounded by the input size.  Garbage collection
hooks can call the module while tagged results or keywords are being converted
to Lisp values without deadlocking.

** NEWS is included in release archives and Nix packages.
The package and native module versions are both 0.2.0.  Rebuild the native
module when upgrading from source.

* Changes in emacs-jieba-rs 0.1.0 (2026-07-20)

** Initial release of the Emacs package and Rust dynamic module.
The package provides precise, full, and search-mode Chinese segmentation,
part-of-speech tagging, and TF-IDF and TextRank keyword extraction.  TF-IDF is
the default keyword extraction method.

** Chinese word and sentence movement and optional visual displays.
'jieba-rs-mode' remaps standard word and sentence movement commands.  Word
boundary and part-of-speech overlays can be toggled independently.  HMM word
discovery, normalization rules, and boundary separators are customizable.

** User dictionaries and source and binary installation are supported.
Words can be loaded from a user dictionary or added during a session, with
optional persistence.  GNU Make and Nix build the native module and Emacs
package.  Release archives include the native module.

